  are tasks worth watching.

  The one thing I can't guess, because it changes the shape of all four layers:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this app has more than one person in it. Single-user and multi-user aren't different sizes of the
  same design — they're different designs.

  - [1mSingle-user[0m buys you no server, no accounts, no auth: tasks live in the browser, and "notification" means a reminder
    or an in-app activity feed you show yourself. Cheap, fast, and it rules out ever notifying someone [3melse[0m without a
    rewrite of the storage layer.
  - [1mMulti-user[0m means a backend, accounts, and real delivery (email/push/websocket) — that's where "tasks they care
    about" is a meaningful phrase, since the interesting case is [3msomeone else[0m touched your task. Costs a server and auth
    up front. Hard to add later: browser-local storage doesn't become multi-user incrementally.
  - [1mSingle-user now, multi-user later[0m is the trap option. It's only honest if we put storage behind an interface from
    the start, and it still means rewriting persistence when you switch.

  [1mMy recommendation: decide this now, and pick single-user unless you know you need sharing.[0m The phrase "users who care
  about tasks" hints multi-user, but your repo is an empty HTML page — and the cost gap between those two paths is
  roughly a weekend versus a month. I'd rather not spend that on an inference.
